### Flexible Candidate Path Selection of SR Policy
* **Presenter:** Isoo Liu
* **Slides:** [Flexible Candidate Path Selection of SR Policy](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-spring-flexible-candidate-path-selection-of-sr-policy-00)
* **Document Reference:** [draft-ietf-spring-sr-policy-flexible-cp-selection](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-spring-sr-policy-flexible-cp-selection/) (*Flexible Candidate Path Selection of SR Policy*)
* **Updates & Discussion:**
  * Isoo Liu detailed updates following adoption:
    * **Information Model (Section 4.2):** Added threshold parameters for jitter/latency under Candidate Paths (CP).
    * **Multiple Segment Lists (Section 4.4):** Defined evaluation rules when individual segment lists fail quality thresholds, re-evaluating aggregated CP metrics over remaining segment lists before toggling CP eligibility.
    * **Performance Measurement (Section 4.5):** Integrated STAMP extensions and IPv6 end-to-end bandwidth measurement.
    * **Wait-to-Restore (WTR) Timer (Section 4.7):** Added configurable WTR interval for quality degradation and recovery.
    * **Operational Considerations (Section 6):** Added randomness, smoothing, and flapping suppression logic.
  * Greg Mirsky noted that STAMP is designed for end-to-end measurements rather than hop-by-hop tracking, suggesting IOAM or alternate marking for hop-by-hop metrics.
  * Rakesh Gandhi questioned whether bandwidth measurements target control plane or data plane metrics and noted IPv6 destination options are processed by destination nodes rather than hop-by-hop.
  * Successful lab and field trials were reported with H3C and ZTE equipment in China Mobile networks. Authors plan to request WGLC after addressing mailing list comments.

