### Distributed Bandwidth Management in Segment Routing Networks using RSVP-TE
* **Presenter:** Pawan Biram
* **Slides:** [Distributed Bandwidth Management in Segment Routing Networks using RSVP-TE](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-spring-distributed-bandwidth-management-in-segment-routing-networks-using-rsvp-te-00)
* **Discussion:**
  * Pawan Biram outlined the rationale for bringing a distributed RSVP-TE control plane bandwidth reservation toolkit to SR networks (SR-MPLS and SRv6) without requiring a centralized controller.
  * The mechanism leverages RFC 8577 procedures: RSVP-TE path/reservation signaling handles per-hop bandwidth admission control, priority preemption, auto-bandwidth, and binding SID delegation while installing standard SR adjacency/End.X SIDs into the forwarding plane. Interoperability between HP and Arcus was demonstrated at the IETF 127 hackathon.
  * Susan Hares clarified that the proposal introduces control plane RSVP-TE signaling without altering BGP or data plane forwarding states.
  * Zafar Ali raised concerns regarding protection mechanism interactions (RSVP facility backup vs. TI-LFA), IGP label ownership, and handling IGP label re-assignments.
  * Greg Mirsky confirmed that IGP-TE advertisement of link attributes is assumed.
  * Tarek Saad expressed concern that limiting bandwidth reservations to strict adjacency SIDs / End.X SIDs restricts operators from using non-strict SR paths (node SIDs and ECMP).
  * Jie Dong suggested that the SPRING working group should first evaluate overall requirements for distributed bandwidth management before selecting specific protocol approaches.
  * Andrew Stone noted potential subtle operational assumptions being broken when standard SR adjacency SIDs are consumed by RSVP-TE LSPs.

