### Distributed Bandwidth Management for Segment Routing
* **Presenter:** Shraddha Hegde
* **Slides:** [Distributed Bandwidth Management for Segment Routing (draft-hegde-spring-sr-distributed-bw-00)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-spring-distributed-bandwidth-management-for-segment-routing-draft-hegde-spring-sr-distributed-bw-00-00)
* **Discussion:**
  * Shraddha Hegde presented the architectural integration of RSVP-TE distributed bandwidth management into the SR Policy framework (RFC 9256).
  * The headend computes paths and triggers RSVP-TE signaling to reserve link bandwidth via IGP-TE accounting. Upon successful signaling, the headend programs standard SR segment lists (adjacency SIDs, End.X SIDs, micro-SIDs). Auto-bandwidth dynamically adjusts segment list bandwidth or container segment list counts.
  * Zafar Ali supported documenting the overall architecture in SPRING as a baseline for protocol-specific drafts in MPLS and TEAS, emphasizing the need to document SID ownership, protection mechanisms, and OAM interactions.
  * Michael Gorokhovsky raised concerns about mixing traffic engineering databases and application-specific link attributes (ASLA) between RSVP-TE and SR applications.

